    "hypothesis": "Where the magnetic and the gravity horizontal gradients are both strong, two independent contrasts share one boundary -- the classical cross-gradient structural argument (Gallardo & Meju 2003). Snapping to that conjunction should be cleaner than snapping to either field alone.",
    "mechanism": "The two rank surfaces are averaged with equal weight, so a cell scores high only if both fields are high there. Dots then climb the shared boundary rather than one field's processing artefacts.",
    "confounder": "Equal weights are a declared choice, not a fitted one; if one field is noisy over part of the footprint the average inherits half of that noise. The mean also allows one strong field to carry a cell, so it is a soft conjunction rather than the strict minimum. The averaged surface is ranked again so that the 0.75 gate is the same percentile on all three variants.",
    "falsifier": "The combo must beat **both** single-field variants by >= 0.005 live to support the conjunction mechanism. If it lands between them, the average is an average and the mechanism is unsupported; inside +/-0.010 nothing is learned."
  },
